                                          "The first usage of the term \"binge-watch\" dates back to 2003, but the concept of watching multiple episodes of a show in one sitting gained popularity around 2012. Let's explore the advantages and disadvantages of binge-watching.\n\nBinge-watching creates a sense of community around a show, called \"a shared cultured area\" by experts. This mutual experience permits viewers to debate and revel in the show with everybody from a coworker to a stranger in line at the grocery store. People who feel lonely and isolated may find it easy and relatable to be in this shared area in which they can also find space to share personal views. Romantic relationships and friendships can be strengthened by binge-watching as a result of the shared interest, since it is not only a fun activity, but a conversation starter and easy way to share time together. Binge-watching can also be therapeutic in times of illness or in mending troubled family relationships. According to psychiatrists, binge-watching releases dopamine within the brain, which creates a sense of enjoyment and facilitates people in relaxing, thus relieving stress. Moreover, finishing a series gives viewers a feeling of control and power, which might be helpful if they are not feeling that in their daily lives.\n\nHowever, according to a recent study, adults who watched over three hours of television daily increased their chance of premature death twofold compared with those who watched less. Sitting for extended lengths of time increases your chance of developing medical conditions including diabetes, heart disease, and cancer, regardless of whether you exercise or not. While binge-watching can be fun and feel fulfilling, there's consistently the peril of becoming too obsessed. Behavior also becomes addictive when it starts to negatively influence different parts of your life. Binge-watchers reported more fatigue, more frequent bouts of insomnia, poorer sleep quality and greater alertness prior to going to sleep. Further analysis found that binge-watchers had a 98% higher likelihood of having poor overall sleep quality compared with those who did not consider themselves to be a binge-watcher.",
