                                    id: "f3-rea-61",
                                    type: "comprehension",
                                    passageTitle: "Reading Passage — Foamed Bitumen and Flood-Resistant Roads",
                                    passage:
                                          "During heavy rainstorms, aside from the dangers of roads becoming incredibly slippery and flooded, potholes begin to emerge, posing a great risk to drivers. Apart from the immediate danger that these holes pose, it normally ends up taking days or even weeks for them to be fixed, meaning the danger is prolonged. However, engineers down in Australia have tested a type of road surface that can deal with hurricane-level rainfall, so maybe it's time to give it a shot on our little island, Malta.\n\nOne of the issues that Malta faces is that when it rains, it rarely tends to be a mild affair anymore, with torrential downpours causing floods which in turn cause some serious damage to our roads. Without wanting to scoff at the damage caused by Maltese rainfalls, they most certainly are not as heavy as some of the hurricanes Australia has had to face. So how do their roads remain relatively intact?\n\n____________ The answer is foamed bitumen stabilization. What happens is that small amounts of air and cold water are injected into hot bitumen, which is a sticky dark substance typically used for road surfaces. This injection produces an instantaneous expansion of the bitumen to about 15 times its original volume, which forms a fine mist of foam. This expansion is due to the water being converted into steam by the hot bitumen. The bitumen now has a water-resistant layer that is not just stronger, but also flexible and can better withstand flooding.\n\nTested on pavements and roads alike, when the flooding receded, both surfaces were left intact which meant fewer repairs needed to be made. So, not only did it make the roads safer in terms of fewer potholes, but it was also easier on the taxpayers' pocket because less work was necessary to fix the damage.\n\nClimate change might be partly to blame for worsening road conditions, as transport minister Ian Borg had explained a while back, but that doesn't mean that we must remain victim to it, as it's giving rise to much innovation.",
