                                    passageTitle: "Reading Passage — Soil Pollution",
                                    passage:
                                          "Soils in different regions of the world are under great pressure according to one of the UN reports on soil pollution. Soils not only provide 95% of humanity's food but are also the largest active store of carbon after the oceans. Therefore, they are crucial in fighting the climate crisis. However, industrial pollution, mining, farming and poor waste management are poisoning soils. ________, the \"polluter pays\" principle is absent in many countries.\n\nPollutants include metals, cyanides, DDT and other pesticides, and long-lasting organic chemicals such as PCBs, the report said. These pollutants make food and water unsafe, which in turn cuts the productivity of fields and endangers wildlife. However, it said most releases of pollutants that end up in soils are not easily quantified, so the true damage naturally remains highly uncertain.\n\nInger Andersen, head of the UN environment programme (Unep), said: \"Soil pollution may be invisible to human eyes, but it compromises the food we eat, the water we drink and the air we breathe. Pollution knows no borders. Contaminants move through soil, air and water. It is time to reconnect with our soils, as it is where our food begins. Soil pollution should no longer be a hidden reality. Let us all be part of the solution to soil pollution.\"\n\nThe future for soils looks bleak, and their state is at least as important as the climate emergency and the destruction of the natural world above ground, according to the scientists behind another UN report on soil biodiversity. Since the Industrial Revolution, about 135bn tonnes of soil has been lost from farmland. As it takes thousands of years for soils to form, urgent protection and restoration of the soils that remain is needed, the scientists said.\n\nAccording to the report, the fundamental step of identifying the ones who are responsible for the pollution is still lacking in many countries. Soil pollution is expected to increase unless there is a rapid shift in production and consumption patterns. A political commitment towards a real sustainable management, where nature is fully respected, is also needed.",
