                                    passageTitle: "Reading Passage — The History of Chocolate",
                                    passage:
                                          "We have the ancient Maya of Mesoamerica to thank for chocolate although they did not make the bars we know and love today. It all starts with cacao beans. The Mayans would dry, grind, and mix the beans with water to create a drink, but this was nothing like today's hot chocolate. In fact, it was quite bitter and frothy and was often mixed with chili. After the Aztecs conquered the Maya, they went crazy for the stuff.\n\nThe Mayans and the Aztecs believed that chocolate was a gift from the gods. The Aztecs in particular revered the drink; they gave it to victorious warriors after combat, employed it during religious rituals, and used cacao beans as currency. To them, cacao beans were more important than gold.\n\nIn the 16th century, Hernán Cortés traveled to Mesoamerica to establish Spanish colonies. Upon arriving, he was greeted with gallons of the spicy drink. He brought some back to Spain, and it became a hit. Initially, it was often used as a medicine, but its bitter taste led people to try sweetening it by adding sugar, vanilla, or honey.\n\n____________ Coenraad van Houten from Amsterdam invented the 'cocoa press,' which separated the fat from cacao beans, leaving behind a fine powder. This powder was much tastier to enjoy as a drink, and people started adding milk to it instead of water, making it more like the hot chocolate we drink today. This method also meant chocolate could be mass-produced; consequently, it became cheaper and more affordable to the wider public.\n\nIn 1847, British chocolatier J.S. Fry and Sons had the novel idea of recombining the fat and liquor and adding sugar. He set this in molds and created the chocolate bar. The chocolate made through this method resembled a mild dark chocolate.\n\nThe next big episode in the chocolate saga came almost 30 years later. In 1875, Swiss chocolatier Daniel Peter put powdered milk in the mix, creating the world's first milk chocolate bar.\n\nChocolate's popularity soared from then on, and it has never really declined.",
