                                          "The International Day of Forests has been celebrated every year on March 21 since its establishment in 2012. ____________ as they are home to about 80% of the world's biodiversity. They are also the second biggest storehouse of carbon after oceans as they absorb significant amounts of greenhouse gasses.\n\nForests not only benefit nature in so many ways, but are also extremely vital to humanity. A quarter of all modern medicines are made from plants that grow in tropical forests, with over 70,000 plant species being used in medicines around the world. An estimated 1.6 billion people rely on forests for their livelihoods. Over 250 million people live in forests. The biodiversity and soil benefits forests provide help increase food stability and availability.\n\nDespite the vast importance of forests for all aspects of life, they are quickly disappearing. Deforestation and expansion of industries such as agriculture and manufacturing have harmed forests worldwide. Deforestation and forest degradation impacts socioeconomically disadvantaged people much more. Areas that are more likely to suffer from forest-related natural disasters typically have lower costs of living. That's why homes in these areas are more accessible to people who may not be able to afford to live in areas less susceptible to these natural disasters.\n\nSubsistence farming, or farming to provide for a family or small group, benefits greatly from the biodiversity and soil quality forests create. Subsistence farmers do not farm in search of profit, but rather to meet their own basic needs. Economically disadvantaged communities in rural areas are more likely to rely on subsistence farming. Therefore, they are usually affected by the changes in water cycles, soil erosion, and biodiversity that forests help regulate.\n\nDespite the many benefits of forests, the world is losing about 10 million hectares of forest each year. This is an area about as big as Iceland. However, hope is not lost. Through reforestation efforts of many governments, deforestation has decreased significantly in Asia and South America since the 1990s. With increased efforts, forests, and the many benefits they provide, can be restored.",
